# Is This the Plan for 1961?

## The DMK and Its Growth - Affection Within the Party

Younger brother,

As soon as the DMK was founded, those accustomed to celebrating the inherited privileges of landed wealth in politics tried to frighten people by saying it would never sprout.

The DMK sprouted, grew, and showed itself in fresh green.

Those who loudly proclaimed that they alone had mastered the rare art of conducting politics predicted that the DMK was a four-day performance that would dance briefly and subside.

Year after year, the DMK continued to grow with renewed radiance.

Those who imagined themselves guides to everyone mocked us: "Seven or eight fellows wander about talking of some party. Who else will join them?"

But farmers and small traders began saying, "The DMK is our place." Students came, declaring that they would join this camp to safeguard the country's honour. Factory workers and people who had distinguished themselves in the fine arts entered, called it their party, and gave it life. Students of history came; many scholars described how Tamils had lived; poets and musicians offered support. The party shone in an attractive form.

The self-styled great men laughed thunderously. "Who will join these people? Five hundred mendicants perhaps! What can come from gathering those who survive by dancing and singing? They may speak entertainingly, apply make-up, and act in plays that make onlookers laugh. Can party comrades conduct an agitation? Can an anklet become a warrior's heroic anklet?" They said no one among us possessed ability or courage.

Party comrades rose in agitation, vowing to give even their blood to wipe away the country's degradation. They endured burning words without weakening. They did not hide for fear of harsh imprisonment. The loss of limbs or eyes did not shake them. Even when precious lives were lost, their courage did not diminish by the smallest measure. Seeing the force of liberation surge forward, the Congress rulers could only wring their hands.

The rulers then adopted the pose of mountain-like wisdom. "What extraordinary courage is this? They go to prison, return, and speak again. An agitation must have proper justification. If they oppose everything, who will respect them? If they have a programme the country can accept, they should explain it properly and win the people's agreement. Why abandon this civil method and shout 'Down with this! Down with that!' over everything?"

The party answered: "Do not imagine that we know only how to go to prison. We also possess the courage that opens the prison doors within people's hearts." It travelled everywhere explaining the struggle for liberation. It called for a moral struggle so that the fallen sacred homeland might rise again and plant the flag of victory. "You ask for our programme. We have one, and within it are many thousands of programmes, as thousands of banyan trees reside within a seed." To make opponents tremble, it raised the cry: "Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians!"

Those hungry for power beat their verbal drums. "A slogan, merely a slogan! Have you heard them? Everything they raise is only theatre. They speak of ancient glory as though they had witnessed it and show a moving picture called Dravida. Are these actors going to rule the country? Is that proper?"

The party defended art and the ideas it could convey. "Drama and cinema are professions, sir, and fine professions capable of capturing the heart. Did the eminent men who now mock artists not seek their service when opposing the British? Artists are not strange objects arriving from elsewhere; they are part of us. They too possess the right to protect their mother tongue. When good people struggle to make the country their own, performers also have work to do." Party members staged plays and delighted both the public and artists.

Congress ministers, proudly imagining the world of art belonged to them, brought in artists dressed in khadi and winked: "See our ability. The party ranks will crumble at this sight."

But renowned artists did not come merely to sing and entertain. They undertook to explain the party's path to others. They studied and understood the excellence of the sacred homeland. They were not dolls that merely nodded; they came forward even to go to prison and did not flee under cover. After they joined, many other artists and workers joined too and stood ready to labour for liberation.

Established artists did not ask what schemes the party would adopt to solve the problems of the art world and improve their position. They said: "Art is our profession, but we remain in it because it too can assist the path shown by our party." Even if a proprietor dressed as Arjuna offered a lakh of rupees, they answered: "If you seek our service, hear this: unless our principles remain unharmed, we shall not act in your film. We do not exist for cinema alone. We aim to see a worthy Dravida." Thus they protected their integrity.

Then came another story: "Leave all this aside. There are countless splits and differences among them! Each man has his own plan and rank. It need only become public. They compare one against another. They will never function together again; watch them tear apart and run!" Malevolent people spoke in this manner.

Madmen, you roam through speeches and writings hunting for differences. A garden of ideas cannot contain only one flower; many colours and varieties bloom there. Is it not foolish to claim enmity among the smiling jasmine, the graceful rose, and the beautiful lotus admired without being plucked? When people work together, many methods must be discussed. Anger may arise. Mischief-makers may magnify it into hurt and revel in delight. Yet the bond created by our ideal, "Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians," will not produce ruptures among us. The party grew under this pledge and terrified the cunning.

Next they said: "These are men with rolling eyes and threatening speech. They will provoke riots and leave corpses behind. They are not people who stop with words; they gather an army to launch a great war, turn the country into a field of blood, and dance like demons. They will not work through righteousness but unleash violence, endanger everyone, and turn the land into a killing field."

We declared that killing people in heaps and planting a flag was not our cruel method. We did not take up sharp swords to stab and tear out entrails. Those opposing us were not foreigners from another land; they too were our siblings, labouring without awareness while wearing chains. Our method was not the hunt in which brothers cut one another down. Though subjected to stabbing and cutting and made to shed blood, party comrades did not descend even slightly into cruelty. They stated their principles and raised the movement until its tears were praised as the country's precious gems and ordinary people embraced it.

Congress members then asked: "Was violence not your method? You fellows escaped; otherwise we would have captured and destroyed the whole lot. Very well, you have said you do not want violence. What other method do you possess? Tell us."

We rejected violence because it was neither proper nor our tradition. The fact that those now leading the party considered it unsuitable did not mean that no one favoured it. There was no shortage of courageous people asking whether liberation could ever come by any means other than violence. Do not imagine that absolutely no one longed to establish camps, wage clandestine war, manufacture explosives, launch rapid attacks, and destroy northern domination, inspired by stirring accounts in histories of liberation struggles.

For the moment, such people allowed the party to remain with those who rejected that method. Neither they nor anyone else could say when the party might pass into their hands. Your own arrogance could hasten that day. Those who seek victory through moral struggle now bear responsibility for the party. If you fail to recognise this truth and continue unleashing arrogant repression, many may rise crying that no method except destruction will work. Today our party has announced to the rulers that it has adopted the method of the vote, not the method of violence.

"Oh! The electoral method, is it? Very well. Show your skill in the field!" Congress members shouted through laughter, confident because the country had crowned them masters of elections. "They will apparently contest elections! Their street-corner speeches provide people with entertainment, nothing more than verbal concerts. Deluded by this, they now think they can stand for election. Poor things, they will be shattered to dust. Grass will grow where the party once stood. The time has come to strike them down and level them to the ground. Jai Hind!" The wielders of authority set out with new confidence.

With opponents surrounding it on every side and wondering what gave it courage, the party entered the elections. It conducted effective propaganda across the country and worked energetically for victory until every home discussed the DMK. No distinction then arose between artists and lawyers. Rajendran of the art world contested in Theni amid fierce opposition. Other artists travelled all day working for the party's success.

The arrogant mocked us: "Winning votes is not a game. We have faced many elections and made mighty kings bite the dust. Will these wooden dolls defeat us? Will the story-writer Karunanidhi, the poet Kannadasan, and Annadurai, who writes screenplays, stand against us?" But the people welcomed and honoured us, voted in our favour, gave the party seventeen lakh votes, and sent our members to the legislature.

The rulers responded: "Somehow they have won, but what does it matter? Power remains with us. Look how these things grin helplessly before us. Many once opposed us; today they wait in penance for our favour. They are politically immature. Speak to them in syrupy language and they will bow at our feet. Show them a job worth a few hundred rupees, a business licence worth several thousand, or some other taste of profit, and they will dissolve their party and fall at our feet. The world knows how the Workers' Party disintegrated. Have you not heard how the Commonweal Party declared its shop closed and sought refuge with us? Wait and see; you will understand the course these people take." Such talk irritated all who heard it.

Party comrades declared: "Even after entering the legislature, we shall not become slaves of power, deviate from principle, or change our purpose. There are many fronts in the struggle to liberate the sacred homeland; this is one. Anyone who thinks there is no more work merely because we sit here is a fool." They travelled with still greater energy, fostering greater and renewed enthusiasm for national liberation.

Permits, licences, contracts, and bribes offer many ways to destroy integrity. Yet to this day no one has been able to point to any stain upon our comrades, who continue their work in purity.

Those who failed to recognise the party as fire beneath ash rejoiced: "The work that scattered sparks is finished. Now they will recline on cushions and talk. They possess no more fighting strength; their hearts have rusted. All their slogans were merely to capture these seats. They will now remain subdued."

Whenever opportunity arose, party comrades rose and proved this false. Their courage turned the black-flag demonstration into an epic. As soon as the danger of Hindi's arrival appeared, the brave cried, "Where? Where?" Opponents had imagined entry into the legislature would weaken our courage and alter our nature. Instead, they were bewildered to find that the party had not lost even the smallest measure of bravery.

They had expected us to perish in many dangerous situations, to fight among ourselves and die. Yet we survived, resolved every dispute that arose over positions, and continued our upward march. Like vultures circling and longing for a corpse to fall, opponents wander about sniffing and watching: "How much longer? When will the split come?"

They wait for the slightest difference among us, ready to enter like a snake through a crack. They understand that the party's present strength arises from the affection within it. If they can somehow destroy that affection, our formation will collapse like a wall stripped of mortar. Convinced that victory lies on this front, opponents roam feverishly and strive to destroy our tradition.

They search daily for some new or ancient problem, philosophical quarrel or smoke, or uncertainty about status. Whatever they find, they intend to magnify before the town and disfigure us. The general election approaches; the party grows. Their superiors ask, "What are you doing? Is this the limit of your ability?" Fearful of losing their rule, Congress members seek some means to disrupt our unity.

Whether we try to alter the party's form, protect discipline and order, devise improved procedures, decide who receives which position or constituency and task, recognise those whose service strengthens us, or select people for leadership, they turn every breath into a cyclone and every word into a great storm. They wish to foster differences, create a great split, shatter the organisation, and then celebrate: "See! For twelve years those fellows strutted proudly and skilfully defeated many attempts, but at last we smashed their organisation. Its scattered members weep. Who can ever build such an organisation again? We may now live without fear."

Knowing this, we must remember the long road we have travelled and how we defeated every danger. Our opponents called us children, a cinema party, a band of performers, people who would flee at the sight of a stick, schemers seeking comfort and pleasure. They did not spare even our families. For years we endured insult after insult, degrading language, questions and mockery about how one man or another had obtained his livelihood. Our hearts suffered countless blows. Those who reflect upon why we bore everything can understand the present situation clearly.

Peeping through the window of the house opposite is a bad habit. Yet it has become our opponents' pastime, and it brings them profit. What lesson should we learn from Congress members who wander about proclaiming that a great disaster has befallen the DMK?

Pandit Nehru laments that factions exist from local Congress committees to the highest level.

The Congress president has issued an instruction: "Do not discuss outside the unpleasant matters that arise within the house."

The Congress is old, marked by time and covered by a screen of grey hair. Even within that party, people recognise factions as dangerous and say they must not exist.

If we consider flowers of philosophy, no two Congress members agree on matters ranging from cooperative farming to collective living. Yet today the affairs of our house are being reduced to the state of crows pecking at fruit knocked down by a hawk. Is that right? Is it proper? Is this the plan we have struggled so hard to find for 1961?

Younger brother, think it over and show us the right path.

Your elder brother,

19 February 1961
